---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import BuildUpLink from "../../components/UnderTheHood/BuildUpLink.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
import Example from "../../components/Example/index"
import Resizable from "../../components/Blog/Resizable.astro"
import Bordered from "../../component-examples/description-list/Bordered.astro"
---

<TechniquePost slug="description-list-leader-lines">
  <section>
    <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Spec sheets, price lists and receipts put the term on the left, the value
      on the right and a dotted line in between. The old ways are a row of dots
      clipped with <code>overflow: hidden</code>, a repeating background, or an
      empty filler element.
    </p>
    <p>
      Side by side also only works when there is room, and a media query knows
      the viewport, not the column the list sits in.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="the-trick">The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Each <code>&lt;dt&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;dd&gt;</code> pair is wrapped in
        a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>, which is valid in a
        {" "}<code>&lt;dl&gt;</code>, so every pair is its own grid
      </li>
      <li>
        The plain list is two columns with <code
          >justify-content: space-between</code
        >: term at the start, description at the end, nothing in between
      </li>
      <li>
        The bordered variants add a third column. The pair's
        {" "}<code>::after</code> is a grid item too. <code>order: 1</code> puts it
        between the term and the description, and the <code>1fr</code> column stretches
        it
      </li>
      <li>
        Its bottom border is the line. <code>dotted</code> only swaps the border style
        and width
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>container-type: inline-size</code> on the list: at
        {" "}<code>45ch</code> or narrower, the term stacks above the description
        and the line goes
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`.list {
  container-type: inline-size;
}

.list > .item {
  align-items: baseline;
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: auto auto;
  justify-content: space-between;
}

.list.bordered.dotted {
  --border-style: dotted;
  --border-width: var(--ui-border-width-thick);
}

.list.bordered > .item {
  grid-template-columns: auto 1fr auto;
}

.list.bordered > .item::after {
  block-size: var(--ui-border-width-thick);
  border-block-end: var(--border-width, var(--ui-border-width))
    var(--border-style, solid) var(--ui-border-color);
  content: "";
  order: 1;
}

.list.bordered > .item dd {
  order: 2;
}

@container (inline-size <= 45ch) {
  .list > .item {
    grid-template-columns: none;
  }

  .list > .item::after {
    content: none;
  }
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>
    <p>
      The solid and the dotted variant. Drag the edge of the dashed box, or use
      the <strong>List width</strong> slider. At <code>45ch</code> or narrower the
      pairs stack, whatever the size of the window.
    </p>
    <Example wide showCode={false}>
      <Resizable label="List width">
        <Bordered />
      </Resizable>
    </Example>
    <BuildUpLink />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="fallback">Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Without container queries the <code>@container</code> block is skipped: every
        pair keeps its row, and the bordered variants their line, at any width
      </li>
      <li>
        The line is an empty pseudo-element, so assistive tech still reads term,
        then description
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
